---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import {
  GHARCHIVE_REPO,
  SCHEDULER,
  GHARCHIVE_DECL,
  GHARCHIVE_STEP,
  HISTORY_DECL,
  CONSOLE_MAIN,
  CONSOLE_VERSIONS,
} from "../data/gharchive";

const description =
  "Build a lake of GitHub activity on magmalake, twice: once by handing a coding agent the skills and a prompt, and once by hand, from pixi and mojoshelf to Orca pipelines, a flare console, a local run and a deploy to AWS.";

const ORCA = "https://github.com/magmalake/orca.mojo";
const SKILLS = "https://github.com/magmalake/skills";

const prompt = `Build me a lake of GitHub activity with magmalake, and an app that shows how
Mojo libraries have grown.

- Make a new pixi project with orca (\`pixi shelf add orca-mojo\`). Use the
  magmalake skills (orca, dista, magmalake-apps), mojoshelf's skills and
  Modular's mojo-syntax.
- gharchive: a daily pipeline that imports each day of the GitHub Archive
  (data.gharchive.org, 24 hourly .json.gz files) into an Iceberg table
  gharchive.events (id, type, created_at, repo, actor, the event's payload
  as JSON), partitioned by day, from 2023-01-01, every day at 03:00 UTC for
  the day before.
- mojo_repos: a sensor on gharchive.events that finds Mojo repositories
  (pushes touching .mojo or .🔥 files, and projects whose pixi.toml or
  mojoproject.toml names a Mojo package) and keeps them in mojo.repos (when
  GitHub says each was created, when it was first seen, how it was found,
  stars, forks, license, default branch, description).
- mojo_history: keyed by repository, 50 a run, started by new repositories:
  clone each one in a dista task and read its history into mojo.commits
  (lines of Mojo at each commit, which tooling the project used: none, then
  magic, then pixi, and the Mojo version its lock file or manifest named)
  and mojo.migrations (each change of tooling or Mojo version). Versions
  sort in one order across Modular's renumbering (24.x, 25.x, 0.25.x,
  0.26.x, 1.x); test that.
- The console: a flare app over those tables: the libraries by size and
  age, the Mojo versions they moved to over time, a page per repository.
- Run it locally first on a few hours of the archive. Then deploy it to AWS
  with \`orca aws\` (on-demand workers, autoscaling from 0), backfill one
  month to see what it costs, then the rest, and serve the console on its
  own hostname. The tables go in the stack's public bucket.`;

const diagram = `GH Archive ──gharchive──▶ gharchive.events ──mojo_repos──▶ mojo.repos ─┐
 (daily, 03:00 UTC,         (by day)         (sensor)      mojo.checked │
  a dista task per hour)                                  mojo.repo_keys┘
                                                               │
                            mojo.commits ◀──mojo_history──────┘
                            mojo.migrations  (keyed by repository, 50 a run,
                                              a dista task per clone)
                                   │
                                   ▼
                          bin/console (flare)`;
---

<Base title="Getting started" description={description}>
  <section class="section section--flush">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide docs">
      <p class="eyebrow">Getting started</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">A lake of GitHub activity</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        This page builds one project two ways. The project is
        <a href={GHARCHIVE_REPO} rel="noopener">gharchive.example</a>: it imports every day
        of the <a href="https://www.gharchive.org/" rel="noopener">GH Archive</a> into an
        Iceberg table, finds the Mojo repositories in it, reads each one's git history, and
        serves a web console that shows how Mojo libraries have grown. Everything in it is
        Mojo, in one pixi project. An agent built it from the prompt below with only the
        skills as documentation, so the first section gives you that prompt, and the second
        walks through the code it wrote and how to run and deploy it yourself.
      </p>

      <nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <a href="#agent">Let an agent build it</a>
        <a href="#skills">Install the skills</a>
        <a href="#prompt">The prompt</a>
        <a href="#steps">Step by step</a>
        <a href="#tools">pixi and mojoshelf</a>
        <a href="#project">The project</a>
        <a href="#code">The code</a>
        <a href="#local">Run it locally</a>
        <a href="#aws">Deploy to AWS</a>
      </nav>

      <pre class="docs-diagram" aria-label="The pipelines and the tables they write"><code>{diagram}</code></pre>

      <!-- ======================== let an agent build it ===================== -->
      <h2 id="agent">Let an agent build it</h2>
      <p>
        A coding agent can write all of this if it knows current Mojo, the magmalake
        libraries and AWS. Each of those comes as a skill, and AWS also as an MCP server.
        The commands below are for Claude Code; any agent that reads
        <a href="https://agentskills.io/specification" rel="noopener">Agent Skills</a> can
        use the <code>npx skills</code> lines instead, which need
        <a href="https://nodejs.org/en/download" rel="noopener">Node.js</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 id="skills">Install the skills</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>magmalake's skills</strong> cover
        <a href={`${SKILLS}/tree/main/orca`} rel="noopener">orca</a>,
        <a href={`${SKILLS}/tree/main/dista`} rel="noopener">dista</a>,
        <a href={`${SKILLS}/tree/main/magmalake-apps`} rel="noopener">magmalake-apps</a>
        (a whole project, its lake, a flare app and <code>orca aws</code>) and
        <a href={`${SKILLS}/tree/main/mojo-data-performance`} rel="noopener">mojo-data-performance</a>.
        In Claude Code:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="Claude Code commands" code={`/plugin marketplace add magmalake/skills
/plugin install skills@magmalake`} />
      <p>In any other agent:</p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`npx skills add magmalake/skills`} />
      <p>
        <strong>mojoshelf's skill</strong> tells the agent how to find tins and add them to a
        project:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`npx skills add mojoshelf/mojoshelf --skill mojoshelf-consume`} />
      <p>
        <strong>Modular's <code>mojo-syntax</code></strong> keeps the agent writing the Mojo
        of today rather than the Mojo it was trained on. It is part of
        <a href="https://github.com/modular/skills" rel="noopener">modular/skills</a>; in
        Claude Code, <code>mojo-skills@modular</code> installs the Mojo skills only:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="Claude Code commands" code={`/plugin marketplace add modular/skills
/plugin install mojo-skills@modular`} />
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`npx skills add modular/skills --skill mojo-syntax`} />
      <p>
        <strong>AWS</strong> publishes the
        <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/agent-toolkit/latest/userguide/quick-start.html" rel="noopener">Agent Toolkit for AWS</a>:
        the AWS MCP Server, through which the agent reads AWS documentation and runs AWS
        calls with your credentials, and a set of AWS skills. It needs
        <a href="https://docs.astral.sh/uv/" rel="noopener">uv</a>, and the AWS CLI signed in
        (<code>aws login</code>) for anything beyond reading documentation. In Claude Code
        one plugin brings both:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="Claude Code commands" code={`/plugin install aws-core@claude-plugins-official
/reload-plugins`} />
      <p>
        Elsewhere, add the server to the agent's MCP configuration and install the skills.
        This is AWS's line for Claude Code with your own credentials; the
        <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/agent-toolkit/latest/userguide/getting-started-aws-mcp-server.html" rel="noopener">setup guide</a>
        has the other agents', and the options for sign-in in the browser and for more than one
        profile. Set <code>AWS_REGION</code> to your project's Region:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`claude mcp add-json aws-mcp '{"type":"stdio","command":"uvx","args":["mcp-proxy-for-aws-cli@latest","https://aws-mcp.us-east-1.api.aws/mcp","--metadata","AWS_REGION=us-east-2"],"env":{}}'
npx skills add aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws/skills`} />

      <h3 id="prompt">The prompt</h3>
      <p>
        Start the agent in an empty directory and give it this. It names the skills to use,
        the three pipelines and their tables, the console, and the order to work in: locally
        on a few hours of the archive first, then on AWS.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="prompt" code={prompt} />
      <p>
        The agent that wrote <a href={GHARCHIVE_REPO} rel="noopener">gharchive.example</a>
        from this prompt ran it locally on two hours of 2024-06-12: 476,227 events (154 MB of
        Parquet), 23 candidate repositories, 4 of them Mojo, and 3 histories read, with 372
        commits and 27 changes of tooling or version. It also kept a list of where the skills
        and libraries were wrong or silent,
        <a href={`${GHARCHIVE_REPO}/blob/main/GAPS.md`} rel="noopener">GAPS.md</a>, and the
        skills have since been corrected from it. Expect your agent to take its own route to
        the same tables; the next section is the route this one took.
      </p>

      <!-- ============================ step by step ========================== -->
      <h2 id="steps">Step by step</h2>
      <p>
        The same project by hand: the tools, a new project with Orca in it, the code the agent
        wrote, excerpted from the repository, and how to run it here and on AWS. To run the
        example as it is instead, clone it, install the two tools below, and skip to
        <a href="#local">Run it locally</a>:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`git clone ${GHARCHIVE_REPO} && cd gharchive.example`} />

      <h3 id="tools">Install pixi and mojoshelf</h3>
      <p>
        <a href="https://pixi.sh" rel="noopener">pixi</a> manages the project's environment,
        the Mojo compiler included. mojoshelf's CLI adds <code>pixi shelf</code>, which finds
        and adds tins:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`curl -fsSL https://pixi.sh/install.sh | sh
pixi global install --channel https://mojoshelf.org/channel mojoshelf`} />

      <h3 id="project">Create the project</h3>
      <p>
        A new project needs Modular's channel, pixi's build preview (tins build with
        pixi-build) and the compiler before it adds Orca. The <code>linux-aarch64</code>
        platform is for the image that runs on AWS Graviton instances later:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`pixi init lake -c https://conda.modular.com/max -c conda-forge && cd lake
sed -i.bak 's/^\\[workspace\\]/[workspace]\\npreview = ["pixi-build"]/' pixi.toml
pixi add "mojo-compiler==1.2.0"
pixi workspace platform add linux-aarch64   # for images on AWS Graviton
pixi shelf add orca-mojo                     # brings dista-mojo, iceberg-mojo, ...`} />
      <p>
        <code>pixi shelf add</code> takes a few minutes and prints every tin's build log as it
        goes; it hasn't hung. gharchive.example also adds <code>postgresql</code> and
        <code>git</code> from conda-forge (<code>pixi add postgresql git</code>), for the local
        database and for cloning repositories inside the image. Its layout is the one the
        magmalake-apps skill describes: programs in <code>bin/</code>, one file per pipeline in
        <code>pipelines/</code>, shared code in <code>src/</code>, built with <code>-I src</code>.
      </p>

      <h3 id="code">The code</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>The project's <code>orca</code> command</strong>, which registers pipelines,
        runs the scheduler and serves the UI, is two lines of its own. Its file must not be
        named <code>orca.mojo</code>, which would hide the <code>orca</code> package:
      </p>
      <p class="docs-file">bin/scheduler.mojo</p>
      <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={SCHEDULER} />

      <p>
        <strong>The gharchive pipeline</strong> is one step, mapped over days from
        2023-01-01, on a cron schedule. The compiler checks the step's input and output types
        against the pipeline. <code>writes=</code> declares the Iceberg table it commits to,
        and <code>Hour</code> is the Dista task that downloads, gunzips and parses one hour of
        the archive:
      </p>
      <p class="docs-file">pipelines/gharchive.mojo</p>
      <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={GHARCHIVE_DECL} />
      <p>
        The step keeps four hours in flight at a time and appends each one as it comes back,
        so a day of about 15 GB of JSON never sits in memory whole. Each append is a snapshot
        that Orca numbers, so a retried attempt finds the hours its earlier attempt committed
        with <code>find_commit</code> and skips them:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={GHARCHIVE_STEP} />

      <p>
        <strong>mojo_repos</strong> is a daily sensor on <code>gharchive.events</code>: each
        day gharchive commits starts a run for that day. It collects candidate repositories
        from the day's events, checks them against GitHub, and appends the Mojo ones to
        <code>mojo.repos</code>. It also appends their keys to <code>mojo.repo_keys</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>mojo_history</strong> is keyed: its partitions are the <code>key</code> column
        of <code>mojo.repo_keys</code>, 50 to a run, and as a sensor it starts runs for keys
        it hasn't seen. Its one step gathers a run's keys and sends every repository to a Dista
        task that clones it and reads its log:
      </p>
      <p class="docs-file">pipelines/mojo_history.mojo</p>
      <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={HISTORY_DECL} />
      <p>
        The keys are <code>owner:name</code>, because an Orca key can't contain
        <code>/</code>. mojo_repos writes them to <code>mojo.repo_keys</code> straight through
        iceberg rather than through <code>orca.tables</code>, and leaves it out of its
        <code>writes=</code>, because Orca 0.3.0 won't register a keyed sensor over a dataset
        that a daily pipeline declares. The scheduler still sees the table's new snapshots and
        starts runs for the new keys.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>The console</strong> is a <a href="https://github.com/magmalake/flare_mlake" rel="noopener">flare</a>
        app over <code>mojo.repos</code>, <code>mojo.commits</code> and
        <code>mojo.migrations</code>. Each page also answers as JSON under <code>/api/</code>:
      </p>
      <p class="docs-file">bin/console.mojo</p>
      <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={CONSOLE_MAIN} />
      <p>
        A route is a function from a request to a response. This one lists, for each month,
        how many libraries moved to each Mojo version, with the versions in one order across
        Modular's renumbering, which <code>tests/test_versions.mojo</code> tests:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={CONSOLE_VERSIONS} />

      <h3 id="local">Run it locally</h3>
      <p>
        Build everything and run the tests. Run the binaries inside the pixi environment,
        with <code>pixi run</code> or in <code>pixi shell</code>: they find zstd's shared
        library through <code>CONDA_PREFIX</code>.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`pixi run build      # build/orca, build/{gharchive,mojo_repos,mojo_history}, build/console
pixi run test       # version ordering and history parsing`} />
      <p>
        The pipelines run on their own without a database. <code>GHARCHIVE_HOURS</code>
        keeps the download to two hourly files of about 100 MB each, and
        <code>MOJO_HISTORY_MAX_KB</code> skips repositories bigger than 100 MB, such as
        modular/modular:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`export ORCA_WAREHOUSE=$PWD/build/warehouse
GHARCHIVE_HOURS=15,17 pixi run ./build/gharchive run --partitions 2024-06-12
pixi run ./build/mojo_repos run --partitions 2024-06-12
MOJO_HISTORY_MAX_KB=100000 pixi run ./build/mojo_history run --partitions @all
PORT=8080 pixi run ./build/console      # http://127.0.0.1:8080`} />
      <p>
        To run them the way they run in production, with the scheduler, the sensors and the
        UI, start a Postgres under <code>build/</code>:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`pixi run initdb -D build/pg --auth=trust --username=postgres -E UTF8 --locale=C
pixi run pg_ctl -D build/pg -l build/pg.log -o "-k $PWD/build -c listen_addresses=" -w start
pixi run createdb -h "$PWD/build" -U postgres orca`} />
      <p>
        <code>scripts/env.sh</code> points <code>ORCA_DATABASE_URL</code> at it, puts the
        warehouse and logs under <code>build/</code> and sets the small slice of the archive.
        Register all three pipelines before loading any data, because a sensor only counts
        snapshots made after it was registered. Then backfill one day:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`pixi shell
. scripts/env.sh
for p in gharchive mojo_repos mojo_history; do ./build/orca locate build/$p --root .; done
./build/orca register ./build/gharchive ./build/mojo_repos ./build/mojo_history
./build/orca scheduler &                 # fires, senses, starts runs
./build/orca ui &                        # http://127.0.0.1:8270 (ORCA_UI_ADDR to move it)
./build/orca backfill gharchive 2024-06-12`} />
      <p>
        The scheduler runs gharchive for the day, which starts mojo_repos for the same day,
        whose new repositories start mojo_history. You can watch it in the UI, or in
        <code>build/logs/&lt;pipeline&gt;/&lt;run&gt;/</code>, and the console from above shows
        the result. Without a token GitHub allows 60 API requests an hour, so set
        <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> if a day has more candidates than that; the ones left over
        are checked first on the next run. Stop the scheduler and the UI with
        <code>kill</code> and Postgres with <code>pixi run pg_ctl -D build/pg stop</code>.
      </p>

      <h3 id="aws">Deploy to AWS</h3>
      <p>
        <code>orca aws</code>, in orca-mojo 0.3.0 and later, deploys the project as one
        CloudFormation stack. It drives the <code>aws</code> and <code>docker</code>
        commands you already have, so sign in with <code>aws login</code> (or set
        <code>AWS_PROFILE</code>) and have Docker running. The stack's Availability Zone
        defaults to <code>us-east-2a</code>; in another Region pass one of its zones with
        <code>--zone</code>. If you signed up through AWS's new experience, your project's
        Region is in AWS Settings, under View all projects, Overview, Additional Info.
      </p>
      <p>
        Inside <code>pixi shell</code>, create the stack, with the email that gets its budget
        alerts and a hostname each for Orca's UI and the console. Then point an A record for
        each hostname at the <code>PublicAddress</code> that <code>status</code> prints. On
        Cloudflare, set the records to "DNS only", so that Caddy on the instance can get
        certificates. The UI also answers at <code>SslipUrl</code> straight away.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`./build/orca aws up --email you@example.com \\
    --hostname orca.example.com --console-hostname mojo.example.com
./build/orca aws status`} />
      <p>
        Commit, then build the linux-aarch64 image and push it to the stack's registry. Put a
        GitHub token in Secrets Manager under <code>orca/</code>, register the pipelines from
        <code>pipelines/</code> with on-demand workers that scale from none to four while
        tasks wait, give the token to mojo_repos, and start the console on its hostname:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`./build/orca aws push
aws secretsmanager create-secret --name orca/github-token --secret-string "$GITHUB_TOKEN"
./build/orca aws register --workers 0..4 gharchive mojo_history
./build/orca aws register --workers 0..4 --secret GITHUB_TOKEN=orca/github-token mojo_repos
./build/orca aws app console`} />
      <p>
        On AWS, <code>GHARCHIVE_HOURS</code> and <code>MOJO_HISTORY_MAX_KB</code> aren't set,
        so runs take all 24 hours of a day and every repository. Runs get the token as a
        variable, and every log Orca writes masks its value. After a code change, commit,
        then run <code>push</code>, <code>register</code> and <code>app console</code> again.
      </p>
      <p>
        The UI is public for reading, and its actions need a login. There's no
        <code>orca aws</code> command for users or backfills yet, so add a user and start the
        first month's backfill on the control instance, through Session Manager:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`aws ssm start-session --target <ControlInstance>      # from orca aws status
sudo docker exec -i scheduler orca user add alice        # reads the password from stdin
sudo docker exec scheduler orca backfill gharchive 2025-01-01..2025-01-31`} />
      <p>
        <strong>What it costs.</strong> The control instance, a <code>t4g.large</code> on
        demand with a 40 GB volume and an Elastic IP, runs all month whether or not anything
        runs; it holds Postgres, the scheduler, the UI, Caddy and the console. Each run starts
        on-demand instances of its own, a <code>c7g.large</code> head and
        <code>c7g.xlarge</code> workers unless you register other types, and
        <code>--workers 0..4</code> keeps an idle run at no workers. Downloads from the public
        bucket that holds the lake are billed to you. The stack emails you at 80% of a monthly
        budget, 100 dollars unless you pass <code>--budget</code>. Check the current prices for
        your Region, and look at what the one-month backfill cost before you backfill from
        2023. When you're done, empty the buckets and run
        <code>./build/orca aws down --yes</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <a href="/s/orca/docs/">Orca docs</a> cover pipelines, the scheduler and the UI in
        full, and the <a href={`${SKILLS}/blob/main/magmalake-apps/references/aws.md`} rel="noopener">magmalake-apps
        skill's AWS reference</a> lists what the stack holds and what to do when a step of
        the deploy fails.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

